nodecode-mflow is a rewrite of vladyslavpatrick's vladyslavpatrick/mflow-hermes:

  https://github.com/vladyslavpatrick/mflow-hermes
  commit 9e6428e78413a0fd7b3afdbb29d5001378acad24
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2025 mFlow (LICENSE)

The original is a portable Agent Plugins v1 package with no code of its own: an
mcp.json pointing at mFlow's hosted MCP server (https://mcp.m-flow.io/mcp),
behind one bearer token from MFLOW_TOKEN, and a skill that teaches a model the
server's snake_case tools and their pitfalls. What was rewritten here is that
whole reach, in Common Lisp: the streamable-HTTP JSON-RPC client itself
(initialize once, the Mcp-Session-Id echoed after, one tools/call per verb,
a JSON or `data:' framed answer read by the reply's id, the session re-taken
once when the server forgets it), the skill's tools regrouped into ten the
model calls by name -- projects, tasks, task creation, task
update, comment, Knowledge Base list and write, coding-agent delegation and
status, and mflow_call for every tool not named above -- and the skill's own
guidance, carried over as the text of (help :mflow): ids not names, labelIds
a full replace, list before creating a doc, an ADR's three sections, supersede
rather than delete, confirm delete_* and transfer_knowledge_base, poll the
coding agent and never in a tight loop, a 401 means the token must be renewed.
The plugin's MFLOW_TOKEN becomes the cell's section: token written inline or
token_env naming the variable that holds it. LICENSE is the repository's
LICENSE, unchanged.

What was left out, and why: the Hermes plugin manifest and the skill's
recipes file, which are pointers and prose for a harness rather than reach,
with the recipes' guidance folded into (help :mflow) instead; the OAuth
sign-in path (authorization_code with PKCE, no refresh grant) because it needs
a browser callback this cell does not open, so a Personal Access Token, the
plugin's own Option B, is the one way in; and Python's MCP client, replaced
by this cell's own client over the image's nlk:http.
